hostapd: keep the Neg-TTLM dialog token across parsing the request

ieee802_11_rx_neg_ttlm_req() stores the request dialog token in
sta->neg_ttlm and then calls ieee802_11_parse_neg_ttlm(), which starts
with os_memset(neg_ttlm, 0, ...). The token is 0 from then on. The
Response goes out with SUCCESS and the real token, and its TX status
callback drops it as "session expired" because the token does not match,
so hostapd_drv_set_sta_ttlm() is never called. The station applies the
map to its uplink; the AP keeps sending downlink on every link.

Measured on 2026-09-23 with a 6 GHz-only map: station uplink 5 GHz +0 B,
AP downlink 5 GHz +60.9 MB against 6 GHz +30.7 MB, and the AP kernel
logged mt7996_mcu_peer_mld_ttlm_req only at the teardown.

Store the token after the parser.

--- a/src/ap/ieee802_11_eht.c	2026-09-23 10:59:57.176908705 +0200
+++ b/src/ap/ieee802_11_eht.c	2026-09-23 10:59:57.188062781 +0200
@@ -3157,9 +3157,12 @@
 	}
 
 	neg_ttlm = &sta->neg_ttlm;
-	neg_ttlm->dialog_token = neg_ttlm_req->dialog_token;
 	ret = ieee802_11_parse_neg_ttlm(hapd, elems.ttlm[0], elems.ttlm_len[0],
 					neg_ttlm, &direction);
+	/* The parser starts with a memset of the whole struct - set the token
+	 * after it, or the TX status callback compares against 0 and never
+	 * programs the driver. */
+	neg_ttlm->dialog_token = neg_ttlm_req->dialog_token;
 
 	if (ret) {
 		wpa_printf(MSG_ERROR, "Invalid TTLM format");
